1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,630
Aarav draws a rectangle, four
centimetres wide and three tall.

2
00:00:03,630 --> 00:00:05,923
Its width grows two
centimetres every second.

3
00:00:05,923 --> 00:00:08,334
Its height grows three
centimetres every second.

4
00:00:08,734 --> 00:00:12,594
So does the area grow by two times
three, six square centimetres every

5
00:00:12,594 --> 00:00:13,494
second?

6
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:16,078
Aarav tested this at
his study table tonight.

7
00:00:16,428 --> 00:00:19,901
The real answer is much
bigger, and it keeps changing.

8
00:00:19,901 --> 00:00:21,271
So what went wrong?

9
00:00:21,771 --> 00:00:26,257
Today we learn the algebra of derivatives:
the derivative of a sum, a product

10
00:00:26,257 --> 00:00:27,329
and a fraction.

11
00:00:27,979 --> 00:00:29,031
Quick reminder.

12
00:00:29,031 --> 00:00:32,016
A derivative tells us how
fast something changes.

13
00:00:32,016 --> 00:00:34,589
We write u dash for how fast u changes.

14
00:00:34,989 --> 00:00:35,889
Start easy.

15
00:00:35,872 --> 00:00:38,693
Two tanks fill with
water at the same time.

16
00:00:38,693 --> 00:00:41,801
One gains two litres a
minute, the other three.

17
00:00:41,801 --> 00:00:43,912
Together, five litres a minute.

18
00:00:44,312 --> 00:00:45,925
That is the sum rule.

19
00:00:45,925 --> 00:00:49,374
The derivative of u plus
v is u dash plus v dash.

20
00:00:49,374 --> 00:00:51,602
For a difference, use a minus.

21
00:00:52,352 --> 00:00:54,721
One more tool from the NCERT book.

22
00:00:54,721 --> 00:00:59,698
The derivative of x to the power n is
n times x to the power n minus one.

23
00:00:59,698 --> 00:01:02,017
So x cubed gives three x squared.

24
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:04,020
Now the product.

25
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:07,065
Let the rectangle have
width u and height v.

26
00:01:07,065 --> 00:01:08,806
Its area is u times v.

27
00:01:09,206 --> 00:01:13,072
In a tiny moment, the width
grows by a small bit, delta u.

28
00:01:13,072 --> 00:01:14,953
The height grows by delta v.

29
00:01:15,353 --> 00:01:17,377
Watch the new area appear.

30
00:01:17,377 --> 00:01:20,484
A strip along the top, u times delta v.

31
00:01:20,484 --> 00:01:23,590
A strip down the side, v times delta u.

32
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:27,163
And one tiny corner,
delta u times delta v.

33
00:01:27,163 --> 00:01:32,464
As the moment shrinks to zero, this
corner shrinks much faster, and vanishes.

34
00:01:32,864 --> 00:01:37,535
So the area grows at u times
v dash, plus v times u dash.

35
00:01:37,535 --> 00:01:39,627
Not u dash times v dash.

36
00:01:40,277 --> 00:01:43,777
Let us turn the picture
into a rule, step by step.

37
00:01:44,177 --> 00:01:45,077
Step one.

38
00:01:45,027 --> 00:01:49,807
The new area is u delta v, plus
v delta u, plus delta u delta v.

39
00:01:50,557 --> 00:01:51,457
Step two.

40
00:01:51,348 --> 00:01:53,729
Divide by the small time, delta t.

41
00:01:53,729 --> 00:01:56,516
Each piece now tells us how fast it grows.

42
00:01:57,266 --> 00:01:58,166
Step three.

43
00:01:58,149 --> 00:01:59,838
Let delta t go to zero.

44
00:01:59,838 --> 00:02:04,138
The corner piece goes to zero too,
because it is small times small.

45
00:02:04,888 --> 00:02:05,788
Step four.

46
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:09,144
The derivative of u v is
u dash v, plus u v dash.

47
00:02:09,144 --> 00:02:12,731
NCERT calls this the Leibnitz
rule, or the product rule.

48
00:02:13,481 --> 00:02:14,520
Back to Aarav.

49
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:16,211
Width four, height three.

50
00:02:16,211 --> 00:02:19,512
Four times three, plus three
times two, is eighteen.

51
00:02:19,512 --> 00:02:20,412
Not six.

52
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:22,909
Now the fraction, u over v.

53
00:02:22,909 --> 00:02:24,690
Think of sharing a pizza.

54
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:26,670
More pizza helps each person.

55
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:27,986
More people hurts.

56
00:02:28,386 --> 00:02:33,107
So the top's change counts as plus, and
the bottom's change counts as minus.

57
00:02:33,107 --> 00:02:37,104
The rule is u dash v, minus
u v dash, all over v squared.

58
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:42,875
It works only when v is not zero,
because we can never divide by zero.

59
00:02:43,625 --> 00:02:44,746
A memory trick.

60
00:02:44,746 --> 00:02:48,964
Low d high, minus high d low,
over the square of what is below.

61
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:50,707
Side by side.

62
00:02:50,707 --> 00:02:53,308
For a sum, the derivatives just add.

63
00:02:53,308 --> 00:02:55,828
u plus v gives u dash plus v dash.

64
00:02:56,228 --> 00:02:58,635
For a product, they do not multiply.

65
00:02:58,635 --> 00:03:02,540
Each part takes a turn to change,
while the other stays still.

66
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:04,069
Your turn.

67
00:03:04,069 --> 00:03:08,870
Is the derivative of x squared times
x cubed equal to two x times three

68
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:09,798
x squared?

69
00:03:09,798 --> 00:03:11,110
Pause and think.

70
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:15,210
No!

71
00:03:14,775 --> 00:03:16,490
That gives six x cubed.

72
00:03:16,490 --> 00:03:21,243
But x squared times x cubed is x to
the power five, so its derivative is

73
00:03:21,243 --> 00:03:23,120
five x to the power four.

74
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:24,670
Remember this.

75
00:03:24,602 --> 00:03:27,200
A product does not give
the product of derivatives.

76
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:28,522
Each part takes a turn.

77
00:03:29,222 --> 00:03:30,912
Now, one worked example.

78
00:03:31,262 --> 00:03:34,899
Find the derivative of x
squared, over x plus one.

79
00:03:34,899 --> 00:03:37,735
Then find its value at x equal to one.

80
00:03:38,235 --> 00:03:39,161
Step one.

81
00:03:39,161 --> 00:03:42,798
The top u is x squared,
so u dash is two x.

82
00:03:42,798 --> 00:03:46,549
The bottom v is x plus
one, so v dash is one.

83
00:03:47,299 --> 00:03:48,199
Step two.

84
00:03:48,077 --> 00:03:51,941
Use low d high, minus high d
low, over the bottom squared.

85
00:03:52,691 --> 00:03:53,670
Step three.

86
00:03:53,670 --> 00:03:59,489
That is two x times x plus one, minus x
squared, all over x plus one, squared.

87
00:04:00,239 --> 00:04:01,139
Step four.

88
00:04:01,119 --> 00:04:02,212
Tidy the top.

89
00:04:02,212 --> 00:04:06,129
We get x squared plus two
x, over x plus one, squared.

90
00:04:06,879 --> 00:04:07,779
Step five.

91
00:04:07,724 --> 00:04:10,736
At x equal to one, that
is three over four.

92
00:04:10,736 --> 00:04:14,846
A tiny step of one thousandth on
the graph gives the same slope.

93
00:04:15,496 --> 00:04:16,960
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:20,618
In the quotient rule, many
students start with u v dash.

95
00:04:20,618 --> 00:04:22,180
The order flips the sign.

96
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:26,367
For our example, the wrong order
gives minus three over four.

97
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:31,143
But the graph is going up at x equal
to one, so the slope must be positive.

98
00:04:31,793 --> 00:04:33,329
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:33,329 --> 00:04:34,967
These rules are tools.

100
00:04:34,967 --> 00:04:39,522
Exam questions use them as one step
inside a bigger calculus problem.

101
00:04:40,122 --> 00:04:41,346
Exam level now.

102
00:04:41,346 --> 00:04:44,539
Use the product rule,
then the quotient rule.

103
00:04:44,539 --> 00:04:45,814
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:51,214 --> 00:04:52,730
The answer is C.

105
00:04:52,730 --> 00:04:53,630
Two.

106
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:57,995
First, f of one is one times
g of one, which is two.

107
00:04:58,495 --> 00:05:03,690
By the product rule, f dash of one
is g of one, plus one times g dash of

108
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:04,590
one.

109
00:05:04,228 --> 00:05:07,104
That is two plus three, which is five.

110
00:05:07,604 --> 00:05:12,111
By the quotient rule, h dash of one
is five times two, minus two times

111
00:05:12,111 --> 00:05:13,749
one, over two squared.

112
00:05:13,749 --> 00:05:15,397
Eight over four is two.

113
00:05:15,997 --> 00:05:17,537
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:17,537 --> 00:05:19,120
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:24,506
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

116
00:05:24,906 --> 00:05:25,806
One.

117
00:05:25,289 --> 00:05:28,692
Why is the derivative of a product
not the product of the derivatives?

118
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:30,492
Two.

119
00:05:30,119 --> 00:05:33,850
Find the derivative of x
plus one, times x plus two.

120
00:05:34,750 --> 00:05:35,650
Three.

121
00:05:35,317 --> 00:05:38,619
In the quotient rule, which
term comes first on top?

122
00:05:39,719 --> 00:05:44,114
Next time: two classes have the same
average mark, but their marks look

123
00:05:44,114 --> 00:05:45,189
very different.

124
00:05:45,189 --> 00:05:47,166
How do we measure the spread?

125
00:05:47,166 --> 00:05:48,241
Mean deviation.
